# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# local dev-server capture logs — hold real request data from local probing
devserver*.log
.devserver*.log

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*
!.env.example
# Committed on purpose: it only ever addresses a local throwaway container, and
# the test suite refuses to run without it. Nothing secret may go in it.
!.env.test

# vercel
.vercel

# agent scratch space, and especially agent git worktrees. A worktree under
# .claude/worktrees/<name> gets its own node_modules, and eslint walks it: the
# run goes from 43 warnings to 1003 errors, none of them in project code.
# Committing this directory would take CI's lint step down with it.
.claude/

# Local run state for the design-review hook: a cache keyed by file, plus one
# state file per question holding a live pid, port and heartbeat. All of it is
# per-machine and none of it means anything on another checkout.
.impeccable/

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

.vercel
.env*
!.env.test

# The design-review skill installs its own agents, hook config and reference
# library here. That is per-machine tooling which happens to land inside
# .github/, not project code, so it does not belong in the repo. Scoped to the
# impeccable payload so real CI workflows still get committed.
.github/agents/impeccable-*.agent.md
.github/hooks/impeccable.json
.github/skills/impeccable/
